== Hybrid KEM

*The concatenation-KDF baseline falls to mauling.* With one Malleable component, `Naive` is BROKEN
in IND-CCA2 on either side: `MaulReplay` changes the ignored byte, asks the decapsulation oracle
for the resulting _different_ hybrid ciphertext, receives the challenge key and wins 200 of 200
trials (control C). The same happens with one Constant component. `Naive` also fails all three
K-CT binding games with one Malleable, Constant or SenderKeyed component, and MAL-BIND-K-PK with one
KeySubstitutable, Constant or SenderKeyed component: 28 of its 70 one-failure squares. This is the
counterexample that motivated hashing ciphertexts (D-01), now executable.

*XOR breaks with two sound components.* `Xor` is BROKEN in IND-CCA2 over two _real_ components:
`MixAndMatch` queries $(c_1^*, c'_2)$ and $(c'_1, c_2^*)$ for self-made $c'_1, c'_2$ whose keys it
knows, and XOR-combines the answers to recover the challenge key (200 of 200). This is the B-XOR
control; it shows the harness finds a break that needs no failed component at all.

*Ciphertext-only binding loses public-key binding.* `CtBound` hashes both keys and both ciphertexts
but no public key. No implemented attack won in IND-CCA2 or any K-CT game with one failed
component, but it is BROKEN in MAL-BIND-K-PK with one KeySubstitutable or Constant component on
either side (`CrossKey`, 200 of 200). An unspecified observation, consistent with the reason
VYRA hashes the public keys.

*The X-Wing-style variant depends on its unhashed component.* `XWingStyle` hashes component 2's
ciphertext and public key (HQC here) but not component 1's. No implemented attack won with
component 2 failed, but with component 1 failed it is BROKEN in IND-CCA2 (Malleable, Constant), in
all K-CT games (Malleable, Constant, SenderKeyed) and in MAL-BIND-K-PK (KeySubstitutable,
Constant, SenderKeyed): 14 of 70 squares, all on the unhashed side. This is consistent with X-Wing
relying on a property of its unhashed ML-KEM component #cite-n("XWING")\; it says nothing about
X-Wing itself, whose unhashed component is real ML-KEM and not a toy model.

*VYRA's combiner.* In all 230 cells with exactly one failed component, across every model, game
and adversary, no implemented attack won (claim check V1; 0 of 70 squares). It is BROKEN only when
_both_ components lose confidentiality (Leaky $times$ Leaky, Constant $times$ Constant, Leaky
$times$ Constant), by `Recompute`, as it must be (claim V2): nothing is left to protect. In every
binding game no attack was found even with both components failed; this is an observation, and it
is what collision resistance of the hash over ciphertexts and public keys would predict. Across all
IND-CCA2 cells judged NO ATTACK FOUND, for every design, the largest observed advantage was 0.21,
well below the detection threshold of 0.34.

