== Composite signatures <sec-sig-results>

*Comparison designs.* `Either` (OR-verification) is BROKEN in EUF-CMA with one Forgeable or one
MessageCollapsing component, and in SUF-CMA even over two real components. `Concat` and `ConcatCtx`
are BROKEN over two real components in STRIP-COMPOSITE, STRIP-STANDALONE and MIX (halves are
standalone signatures and are not bound to the composite key); `Concat` also in CROSS-SUITE and
CROSS-CONTEXT; both in S-CEO with one KeySubstitutable component. These are by-design weaknesses
named in control S-B.

*A weakness in our own earlier design.* In VYRA's first composite, the Ed25519 half was PureEd25519
over $M'$. `VyraPureEd` reproduces that design. Its Ed25519 half is accepted by a standalone
verifier in every pattern (STRIP-COMPOSITE, `StripHalf-2`, 200 of 200 already with both components
real). Worse, once ML-DSA is Forgeable, it is BROKEN in STRIP-STANDALONE in 200 of 200 trials by an
adversary that holds only a _standalone_ Ed25519 signing oracle on the composite's Ed25519 key: it
asks that oracle to sign $M'$, forges the ML-DSA half from the model, and assembles a valid
composite. All four oracle modes win (`Recombine-Default`, `-EmptyCtx`, `-ForeignCtx`,
`-SuiteCtx`), the last three because the PureEd25519 half ignores any context. The current design
signs the Ed25519 half as Ed25519ph under the composite context; with the same components and
adversaries, `Vyra` shows no win in STRIP-COMPOSITE or STRIP-STANDALONE in any one-failure pattern
except the context-ignoring one discussed below. `VyraPureEd` stays in the matrix as regression
control S-E. A deployment in which the Ed25519 key also signs elsewhere is a key-reuse setting, the
signature analogue of the reuse that GHP18-style KEM analyses exclude #cite-n("MSS26")\; the finding is small in principle, but
it was a real defect in a design we were about to publish. @fig-sigfix shows the attack and the fix.

// Source: paper/figures/sig-fix.svg, drawn by tools/figures/gen_figures.py from results/.
#figure(
  placement: bottom,
  scope: "parent",
  caption: [The STRIP-STANDALONE finding, before and after. ML-DSA is Forgeable and the adversary holds a standalone Ed25519 signing oracle on the composite's Ed25519 key. Large counts: `Recombine-Default`; small print: all four oracle modes.],
  image("../figures/sig-fix.svg", width: 100%),
) <fig-sigfix>

*Strong unforgeability does not yield.* `Vyra` with one Malleable or one Forgeable component is
BROKEN in SUF-CMA (`Maul`, 200 of 200): keeping the honest half and mauling or re-forging the other
gives a new valid signature on an old message. This was stated in advance as a gap of the
composite (claim `SUF-gap3`): an AND-composite is strongly unforgeable only if both components are.

*Context-ignoring components remove separation.* `Vyra` with one ContextIgnoring component is
BROKEN in STRIP-COMPOSITE in both pairs: that half _is_ a standalone signature. This is why VYRA
treats context binding as a tested contract of every component (RULES 30); the engine does not
enforce it for custom assemblies.

*VYRA's composite with one forgeable component.* With exactly one Forgeable component, no
implemented adversary won against `Vyra` in EUF-CMA, STRIP-STANDALONE (all four modes), MIX, S-CEO,
MBS, CROSS-SUITE or CROSS-CONTEXT (claim check over 44 cells), and no EUF-CMA forgery was found with
one component broken under any other model (30 cells). Over the transition pair, 6 of the 90
one-failure squares of `Vyra` are BROKEN: the four SUF-CMA squares and the two ContextIgnoring
STRIP-COMPOSITE squares just described.

