== Threats to validity <sec-threats>

These limits come from the project's own review of the experiment (`docs/DECISIONS.md`, reviewer
notes) and from re-reading the matrix for this paper.

+ *Most cells are observations.* 2606 of 3365 cells (77%) carry no pre-stated expectation, mostly
  comparison designs and both-failed patterns. Their verdicts were not predicted; reading them as
  confirmed hypotheses would be circular.
+ *The negative control is blind to real-component binding.* Control B ("both real $=>$ no win" in
  the binding games) can fail only through a harness bug, because no adversary targets binding
  weaknesses of real ML-KEM or HQC; the literature analyses those separately #cite-n("Sch24", "KSW25").
+ *Only large advantages are visible.* At $n = 200$, IND-CCA2 needs advantage $>= 0.34$ for BROKEN;
  zero wins in a search game bound the success probability only below 0.032. Apart from the two
  single-win cells below, every BROKEN cell was won in all of its trials, so the experiment shows
  gross attacks only; a subtle one could not have shown up.
+ *Adversary selection.* Adversaries are game-specific. Some NO ATTACK FOUND squares for doubly
  broken designs reflect which strategies were implemented, not resistance.
+ *Single-win verdicts.* In search games one win is BROKEN. Two comparison cells are BROKEN on 1
  win in 200: `Concat` in SUF-CMA under MessageCollapsing $times$ MessageCollapsing (`Forge`) and
  `Either` in CROSS-CONTEXT under MessageCollapsing $times$ Real (`SwapContext`). Both are real,
  challenger-verified wins against the toy models; we have not analysed them further, and an
  incidental collision of the model's 12-bit digest would explain them.
+ *The own-context oracle is excluded.* STRIP-STANDALONE refuses an oracle that signs under the
  composite's own context, because its output is by definition a composite half. A deployment that
  lets a component key sign caller-chosen contexts can therefore still produce composite halves;
  VYRA's only defence is the rule not to use component keys outside the composite.
+ *Small $n$ for dual-PQ*, two designs only, and no SUF-CMA game for that pair.
+ *Idealised failure models and a fixed scope.* A real break will fail in its own way. Only the
  smallest parameter sets were run (the components of VYRA-KEM-1, VYRA-SIG-2 and VYRA-SIG-PQ1); the
  other suites share the composition code but were not tested. MAL binding is
  run only in scenario I; a validity-oracle model was omitted on purpose (`vyra-yield` crate
  documentation). Nothing here models quantum adversaries, side channels, multi-user settings or
  key reuse beyond STRIP-STANDALONE.
